file @compileFor(!ARCH_WASM32);
// @kama/rmlui — RmlUi 6.3 (an HTML/CSS-like UI library) for kama, as a GENERAL binding: contexts, documents from a file
// or from text, fonts, provided images, injected input, elements found by id or selector and read or written, and an
// event queue kama polls. RmlUi draws nothing: `Context.render` records draw commands for whatever renderer the
// program has (csrc/kama_rmlui.h, "THE SHAPE, AND WHY").
//
//   RmlUi        the library: start it, load fonts, provide images, read RmlUi's log and the textures, the last
//                event's parameters, the clipboard; shut it down
//   Context      one UI surface: size, input, update, render, the recorded commands, its event queue; destroy it
//   Document     a loaded document: show, hide, close, find elements, tab order, bindings
//   Element      any element: tag, id, attributes, classes, inline properties, inner RML (set or appended), text,
//                children (counted, removed), a form control's value, its box, scrolling, focus, click, listeners
//   Binding      an id held across rewrites: the same handle for (document, id), re-finding its element and carrying
//                its listeners to it
//   Event        one queued event: the listener (and binding) that heard it, its type, the element, the target
//   Command      one recorded draw (or clip-mask) command; Texture, one font atlas or provided image
//
// EVERYTHING IS A VALUE OVER AN INTEGER HANDLE, AND HANDLES ARE SAFE TO HOLD. RmlUi owns the objects, on the C side;
// these are copyable names for them, with EXPLICIT lifetimes (`RmlUi.shutdown`, `Context.destroy`,
// `Document.close`) — the trade a window or an audio device makes, and the one that lets a program keep its UI in a
// module `static` (which may own nothing). The shim tracks each element with RmlUi's ObserverPtr and each handle with
// a generation, so a handle whose object is gone — a rewrite, a closed document, a destroyed context, a shut-down
// RmlUi — is DEAD: getters answer None / "" / 0 and setters do nothing, never a read of freed memory. Holding one too
// long is a logic question, not a memory one.
//
// NATIVE ONLY. RmlUi (vendored, third_party/rmlui) is compiled for native targets, so every file here is
// `@compileFor(!ARCH_WASM32)` and every `csources` entry carries the same gate. In a browser, @kama/ui puts the same
// documents in the DOM instead.

import { std::collections::ConstView };
export {
    RmlUi, RmlUiError, Context, Document, Element, Binding, Listener, Event, EventType, Command, CommandKind, Texture,
    Vertex, Rect, BoxArea, Modal, Focus, LogLevel, LogEntry, encodeRml,
    LISTEN_BUBBLE, LISTEN_CAPTURE, LISTEN_STOP,
};

